# Device address space

A device address refers to a buffer memory.
The buffer memory are pull out from a pool.
The pool is readble and writeable.

A device address pointer/object/ref to variables.

device float4 *color;

struct Foo
{
    float a[3];
    int   a[3];
};

device Foo *info;

NB:
    textures do not need device attribute. Textures elements are not addressable
    one needs built-in functions.

# Constant adress space

The same device address but they are read-only. Constant attribute behaves like
const in C.

# Thread address space

this refers to per-thread memory address space. They are not visible to others
threads. Variables inside a graphics or kernel function are allocated in the
thread memory space.

# Threadgroup address space

A GPU compute unit(?) can execute multiple threads concurrently in a threadgroup,
and a GPU can execute a different threadgroup for each of its compute units.

Threadgroup memory is faster than device memory. Use the threadgroup address
space to:
- Allocate a threadgroup var in a kernel, mesh, or object function.
- Define a kernel, fragment, or object function parameter that is a pointer to
  the threadgroup address

Threadgroup variables in a kernel, mesh, or object function only exist for the
lifetime of the threadgroup that executes the kernel.

Threadgroup variables in a mid-render kernel function are persistent across
mid-render and fragment kernel functions over a tile.

kernel void
my_kernel(threadgroup float *sharedParameter [[threadgroup(0)]],
…)
{
    // Allocate a float in the threadgroup address space.
    threadgroup float sharedFloat;
    // Allocate an array of 10 floats in the threadgroup address
    space.
    threadgroup float sharedFloatArray[10];
}

NB:
    it seems a GPU has several compute units.

## SIMD- and Quad-groups

Within a threadgroup, you can divide threads into SIMD-groups, which are
collections of threads that execute concurrently. The mapping to SIMD-groups is
invariant for the duration of a kernel’s execution,across dispatches of a given
kernel with the same launch parameters.

In addition, all SIMD-groups within a threadgroup needs to be the same size,
apart from the SIMD-group with the maximum index, which may be smaller, if the
size of the threadgroup is not evenly divisible by the size of the SIMD-groups.

NB:
    A quad-group is a SIMD-group with the thread execution width of 4.

# Threadgroup imageblock address space

This address space refers to objects allocated in threadgroup memory that are
only accessible using an imageblock<T, L> object.
























